== ON THE CONCLUSION OVERREACH ==

You're right. "Strong cryptographic guarantees" in the final paragraph slides from the firehose to the whole system, and that's a category error. The firehose has tamper-evidence, chain continuity, and dual-level attribution (actor + origin). The projections inherit exactly none of that cryptographically. They inherit it insofar as the dispatch logic in core/dispatcher.py and the apply_* methods in board_projection.py and global_projections.py are correct — which is a software correctness claim, not a cryptographic one.

The projection architecture does have mitigations that are worth naming, even though they don't change the category: idempotent applied-event tracking (each event is applied exactly once via INSERT OR IGNORE into applied_events), and full rebuildability (projections can be cleared and replayed from the firehose at any time via Dispatcher.rebuild_all). So a projection bug is recoverable in a way that a firehose integrity break is not. But "recoverable" is still a software claim. The conclusion should have said: the firehose has strong cryptographic guarantees; the projections are derived state whose correctness depends on projection logic, and whose consistency can always be rederived from the firehose.

== ON "DETERMINISTIC" AND CANONICAL LEGACY METADATA ==

This is the one I get to be more precise on, because I went back and read core/migration.py more carefully after your push.

The canonical encoding (encode_canonical_legacy_post_metadata) is a pure function with no implicit serialization. Every field is explicitly packed with struct.pack using fixed big-endian byte order: >Q for unsigned 64-bit, >q for signed 64-bit, >B for booleans, >i for signed 32-bit, >I for unsigned 32-bit length prefixes. String fields are UTF-8 encoded with explicit length prefixes. The field order is fixed in the function body. The docstring states: "No locale-dependent conversion or SQLite row serialization enters this."

So at the function level, canonicalization is fully specified. Two operators calling encode_canonical_legacy_post_metadata with the same inputs will provably get the same bytes, and therefore the same message ID.

The question you're really asking is: can the inputs differ? The inputs come from two sources:

1. SQLite metadata rows (via the AME ORM layer). SQLite stores TEXT as UTF-8 regardless of platform, so the same database file will yield the same string bytes on any platform. The code also normalizes NULL to empty string (post.tags or "", post.subject or "", etc.), so NULL-vs-empty-string ambiguity is resolved deterministically.

2. Flat-file post bodies (via Board._read_content). These are read with encoding="utf-8" and re-encoded with .encode("utf-8"). Python's utf-8 codec does not strip BOMs (that's utf-8-sig), so a BOM would be preserved through the round-trip. This is deterministic.

The edge case that could cause two operators to disagree is not in canonicalization but in data integrity: if the same logical record had been stored differently in the legacy DB — through a schema migration that changed column types, a collation change that altered text representation, or file-level corruption — then the inputs would differ and the message IDs would differ. But that's a data integrity problem upstream of canonicalization, not a canonicalization specification gap.

So my revised claim: canonicalization is specified tightly enough that two independent migrations from the same legacy database will provably agree. Two independent migrations from different copies of a legacy database that has undergone uncontrolled schema evolution might not agree — but in that case, the firehose's equivocation detection (event_conflicts table) would catch the fork after the fact. You're right that catching a fork isn't the same as the fork never happening. The design's answer to that is: the fork can't happen silently, and the migration progress tracking (MigrationProgress) makes a restart idempotent, so a single operator re-running their own migration will never fork themselves.

